@HostAddress = http://localhost:5125

### 0. Service info — lists the available endpoints
GET {{HostAddress}}/
Accept: application/json

### 1b. Brands — A WHOLE COLLECTION IN ONE CALL: mapper.Map<List<BrandDto>>(brands).
#     This endpoint used to read brands.Select(b => b.Map<BrandDto>(mapper)).ToList(), which is
#     the line every application ended up writing in every list endpoint. The destination SHAPE
#     is the type argument now, so BrandDto[], HashSet<BrandDto> and IReadOnlyList<BrandDto>
#     work the same way — and mapper.MapToBrandDtoList(brands) is the typed route that walks
#     no typeof chain at all.
#
#     FIRST, LOOK AT "aliases". Six of the eight seeded brands have a NULL Aliases column, and
#     every one of them comes back as [] rather than null. That is the NULL-COLLECTION POLICY,
#     and it is why BrandDto.Aliases can be declared non-nullable: no caller of this DTO ever
#     writes dto.Aliases?.Count, and the first place somebody would have forgotten to is a
#     NullReferenceException in production rather than a compile error. Request 1b2 asks the
#     database the same question and gets the same answer.
#
#     Then three things that were true before and still are. "isoCode": the entity spells it
#     ISOCode and the DTO spells it IsoCode, so this is case-insensitive matching working end
#     to end, through the database. "foundedYear": quoted, because the entity holds it as an
#     int and the DTO as a string — ShiftMapper converted it, invariantly, on its own.
#     "tags": the entity holds a List<string>, the DTO an IReadOnlyList<string>, and the DTO
#     gets its OWN copy rather than a read-only window onto the list EF is still tracking.
#
#     And "externalIds", which is the point of SM0010: the entity holds them as long and the
#     DTO declares them int, so any id past int.MaxValue maps to a different number — no
#     exception, nothing in the code to say so. That is why SM0010 is a build WARNING.
#     The seeded ids sit inside int range, because this same BrandDto is also PROJECTED —
#     directly in 1b2, and four levels down in 2 — and SQL REFUSES the overflow rather than
#     wrapping it: "Arithmetic overflow error converting expression to data type int". Worth
#     knowing on its own: the conversion that quietly returns the wrong number in C# is a hard
#     error in the database.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands
Accept: application/json

### 1b2. The SAME list, built by the DATABASE — db.Brands.ProjectTo<BrandDto>(mapper).
#     Run it straight after 1b and compare. Every brand comes back identical, "aliases"
#     included, which is the part worth checking rather than assuming: the two backends answer
#     that question in completely different code. In memory it is a ValueConverter builder that
#     substitutes an empty list; in SQL it is a coalesce spliced into the projection. Request
#     1b3 shows what that becomes.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/projected
Accept: application/json

### 1b3. The SQL behind 1b2 — and the one line to find in it:
#
#         COALESCE([b].[Aliases], CAST(N'[]' AS nvarchar(max)))
#
#     The null-collection policy is enforced BY THE DATABASE. EF read the column exactly as it
#     did before and applied the guard in the SELECT list, so the policy costs a COALESCE and
#     not a round trip.
#
#     WHY IT IS NOT ON EVERY COLLECTION, which is the interesting half. Look at [ExternalIds]:
#     it goes through OUTER APPLY OPENJSON with no coalesce anywhere near it. EF recognises a
#     primitive collection by the SHAPE of the expression around it, and `x ?? empty` is a shape
#     it cannot see through — guarding that one would have turned a working query into a
#     runtime "could not be translated", to protect against a null the type says cannot happen.
#     So the guard is written only where the source property is declared NULLABLE, which is the
#     developer's own statement about the column. Declare a collection nullable when it really
#     is, and both halves of ShiftMapper agree.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/projected?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1b4. One brand — TWO doors in one endpoint.
#
#     First mapper.MapOrNull<BrandDto>(brand), for a row that may not be there. Plain Map would
#     THROW on a null source, and deliberately so: asking to build a DTO out of nothing is
#     almost always a bug, and one far cheaper to hear about at the mapping call than three
#     layers away. "The row was not found" is the exception — it is ordinary data — so
#     MapOrNull is the door for it, and it saves every such endpoint writing
#     `brand is null ? null : Map(brand)` by hand. See 1b5 for the null path.
#
#     Then the other overload, brand.Map(existingDto, mapper), which copies onto an object that
#     already exists. "updatedInPlace" proves it is the same instance, and
#     "injectedDependency" proves AppMapper really is a DI service with a dependency.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 1b5. A brand that is not there — MapOrNull hands back null and the endpoint returns 404,
#     with no exception thrown or caught anywhere along the way. That is the whole reason it
#     exists: the absence is a value the endpoint can test, rather than control flow.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/9999
Accept: application/json

### 1c. Stocks — the same generated maps, one object at a time, called DIRECTLY on the
#     mapper instance (mapper.Map<StockDto>(stock)) rather than through a collection overload
#     or an extension method. All three spellings run the same generated code; pick whichever
#     reads better where you are.
#     Look at "bayNumbers": the entity holds List<int>, the DTO IReadOnlyList<string>, so both
#     the collection shape and the element type were converted — and the POST below reads them
#     back the other way.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Accept: application/json

### 1c2. One stock — the instance form of the update overload, mapper.Map(stock, existing)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c3. Create a stock — the REVERSE map, StockDto -> Stock. Every request above goes
#     entity -> DTO; this one only compiles because AppMapper chains .ReverseMap().
#     The id below is ignored — the endpoint resets it so the database assigns the real one.
#     It is also the direction that has to READ the id back out of text: Stock.Id is an int
#     and StockDto.Id is a string, so the reverse map parses it. Leaving "id" out sends
#     empty text, which reads back as 0; send "id": "not-a-number" to watch it refuse.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duhok Distribution Centre",
  "city": "Duhok",
  "code": "DHK-DC",
  "bayNumbers": ["1", "2", "3"]
}

### 1c5. THE MAP HOOKS — BeforeMap and AfterMap, and the order they run in.
#
#      Same shape as AutoMapper's: an Action<TSource, TDestination> chained onto the CreateMap.
#      They become an ordinary statement in the generated method, around the assignments:
#
#          StockHookDto destination = new StockHookDto { };
#          Customizations.RunBefore(source, destination);   // BeforeMap
#          destination.Name = source.Name;
#          destination.City = source.City;
#          Customizations.RunAfter(source, destination);    // AfterMap
#
#      READ "nameSeenByBeforeMap" IN THE FIRST OBJECT. It says "(nothing yet)":
#
#          "afterOneMap": {
#            "name": "Central Warehouse",
#            "nameSeenByBeforeMap": "(nothing yet)",   <- the destination WAS empty
#            "summary": "Central Warehouse (Erbil)",   <- AfterMap saw the finished values
#            "timesMapped": 1
#          }
#
#      "BEFORE" IS MADE GENUINELY BEFORE, not merely called first. A destination has to exist to
#      be handed to you, so every member the generator can assign afterwards is moved OUT of the
#      object initializer. Constructor arguments, init-only and required members are the
#      exception — they cannot be assigned later — so a hook finds those already set.
#
#      AND THE SECOND OBJECT IS THE UPDATE OVERLOAD, mapped twice onto one instance:
#
#          "afterMappingTwiceOntoOneObject": {
#            "nameSeenByBeforeMap": "Central Warehouse",  <- found it already set
#            "timesMapped": 2                             <- both hooks ran again
#          }
#
#      Nothing is arranged on an update: the object arrived built, so the hook runs before the
#      first assignment exactly as you passed it.
#
#      WHEN NOT TO REACH FOR ONE. AfterMap is the useful half — it is for a value derived from
#      the FINISHED destination, like "summary" above, which no MapFrom could produce because a
#      MapFrom sees the SOURCE. Anything derivable from the source alone belongs in a ForMember
#      with MapFrom, which keeps the projection.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/hooks
Accept: application/json

### 1c6. AND WHAT A HOOK COSTS — the map loses its projection, said at BUILD time:
#
#        warning SM0018: the map from 'Stock' to 'StockHookDto' runs BeforeMap and AfterMap
#                        over its destination, so ProjectTo cannot use it; Map is unaffected
#
#      A projection is ONE EXPRESSION handed to the database; there is no statement in it for
#      your code to be. Leaving the projection in place and silently NOT running the hook is the
#      one outcome this library refuses, so asking anyway gets a sentence:
#
#        "ShiftMapper: the map from 'Stock' to 'StockHookDto' runs BeforeMap and AfterMap over
#         its destination, which runs in C# and has no SQL. Use Map instead, or move what the
#         hook does into a ForMember, which projects."
#
#      As of SM0037 the call site is flagged too, so you learn this where you wrote the query
#      rather than only where the map is declared. The endpoint's own ProjectTo carries a
#      #pragma saying the refusal is the point here.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/hooks?project=true
Accept: application/json

### 1c4. Read back what 1c3 created (id 5 on a fresh database) — both directions of the
#     round trip come from that one CreateMap line.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/5
Accept: application/json

### 1c5. Validation — a stock with no name returns 400 before any mapping happens
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "city": "Nowhere",
  "code": "NON-E"
}

### 1d. Products — PROJECTION, and the shortest demonstration of what that buys you.
#     ProductDto carries a BrandDto and a StockDto, and the endpoint contains no Include at
#     all. EF works the joins out from the map itself, so the response comes back three levels
#     deep from one query that reads only the columns the DTOs use.
#
#     Look inside for every earlier feature still working through the nesting: the brand's
#     "foundedYear" is a string over the entity's int, its "tags" an IReadOnlyList<string>
#     over a List<string>, the stock's "id" a string over an int, its "bayNumbers" strings
#     over ints. Add ?sql=true to see the query.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1e. DICTIONARIES — the collection shape that used to be a flat SM0002.
#
#     There is no database in this request, on purpose. A dictionary is how a KEYED PAYLOAD
#     arrives — a price per SKU, a note per line number — and that is where it is worth
#     showing, rather than inside a table it would need a value converter to live in.
#
#     One CreateMap<SupplierFeed, SupplierFeedDto>() covers all three properties below, and
#     each demonstrates a different half of the feature:
#
#       "prices"  the VALUES converted:  999.5 -> "999.5", invariant, so it is the same text in
#                 Baghdad and in Berlin. The dictionary is REBUILT to do it — generics are
#                 invariant, so a Dictionary<string, decimal> never becomes a
#                 Dictionary<string, string> however freely a decimal converts to text.
#       "notes"   the KEYS converted:    1 -> "1". This is the pair the build reports as
#                 SM0008, because converting keys is the one thing a dictionary can lose that a
#                 list cannot: two source keys can arrive as one, and the later wins. It cannot
#                 actually happen for int-to-string, and the note does not pretend otherwise
#                 — the rule is about converting keys, not about these two types. Narrow long
#                 keys onto int and the same note arrives as a real SM0010 warning.
#       "extras"  ABSENT from the request body, and {} in the response. The null-collection
#                 policy again, on a dictionary rather than on a list.
#
#     "ownsItsOwnData" is the other rule every collection follows: the DTO holds a NEW
#     dictionary, not a second reference to the request object's.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "supplier": "Acme Distribution",
  "prices": {
    "APL-IP15P": 999.5,
    "SNY-PS5S": 499
  },
  "notes": {
    "1": "backordered",
    "2": "ships Monday"
  }
}

### 1e2. The same feed with EVERY dictionary left out. All three come back as {} rather than
#     null, so a caller of SupplierFeedDto never tests one for null. Send
#     "extras": null explicitly and the answer is the same — the policy is about the value,
#     not about whether the property was present in the JSON.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "supplier": "Empty Feed"
}

### 1e3. A LIST of feeds — mapper.Map<List<SupplierFeedDto>>(feeds).
#     The collection overloads are generated for every declared map, not only for the ones with
#     a table behind them. Note the second feed sends no prices and the first sends no notes,
#     and both come back with {} for the one they left out.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview-many
Content-Type: application/json

[
  {
    "supplier": "Acme Distribution",
    "prices": { "APL-IP15P": 999.5 }
  },
  {
    "supplier": "Globex Supply",
    "notes": { "7": "arriving late" }
  }
]

### 1f. RECORDS — a destination that is not `new T { }`, which until now was SM0004.
#
#     ProductSummaryDto is a POSITIONAL RECORD, and BrandSummaryDto inside it is another. Neither
#     has a settable property: every value arrives as a constructor argument. ShiftMapper picks
#     the constructor and matches its parameters exactly as it matches members — by name, with
#     the same conversions, and with a nested map where a CreateMap exists:
#
#         return new ProductSummaryDto(
#             source.Id, source.Name, source.Sku,
#             ValueConverter.ToInvariantString(source.Price),   // decimal -> string
#             MapToBrandSummaryDto(source.Brand));              // a record inside a record
#
#     Note "price" comes back quoted and "isoCode" is filled from the entity's ISOCode — both
#     the conversion table and the case-insensitive fallback reach a constructor argument.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/summary
Accept: application/json

### 1f2. AND IT PROJECTS, which is the reason records were worth doing rather than a convenience.
#
#         SELECT [p].[Id], [p].[Name], [p].[Sku], CONVERT(varchar(100), [p].[Price]),
#                [b].[Id], [b].[Name], [b].[ISOCode]
#         FROM [Products] AS [p]
#         INNER JOIN [Brands] AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId] = [b].[Id]
#
#     Compare it with request 1d, the same query into an ordinary class. Same joins, same
#     columns. The shape of the DTO changed and the query did not, which is the whole claim —
#     EF is handed one `new` with real arguments rather than an object initializer.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/summary?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1g. REQUIRED MEMBERS — and both backends side by side so they can be compared.
#
#     `required` is a compile-time rule rather than a strong word: C# REFUSES an object
#     initializer that leaves one out. So an unmapped required member does not leave a property
#     empty, it stops the destination being built at all — and the build says which one:
#
#         warning SM0014: no Map method was generated to create 'InvoiceReceiptDto' because its
#                         required member 'LineCount' (string) is not mapped
#
#     Try it: delete either ForMember in AppMapper and watch the create half of that map
#     disappear with that message, instead of the build failing with a CS9035 inside a generated
#     file you cannot open.
#
#     "agree" is the interesting field. LineCount is required AND customized, and a customized
#     member is normally ABSENT from the generated projection — the expression lives in
#     AppMapper.cs and Compose splices it in at run time. A required one cannot be absent from a
#     template that is itself compiled, so the generator writes `LineCount = default!` and Compose
#     replaces it. If that ever stopped working these two objects would differ and nothing else
#     would look wrong. Request 1i is the same endpoint read for the OTHER thing it demonstrates.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/receipt
Accept: application/json

### 1h. CONSTRUCTUSING — for the case convention cannot reach.
#
#     InvoiceLabelDto has no parameterless constructor and no settable Label, and no constructor
#     ShiftMapper could pick would know about the invoice-numbering service. So AppMapper supplies
#     the construction itself:
#
#         .ConstructUsing(s => new InvoiceLabelDto(_numbering.Prefix + s.Number))
#
#     It replaces CONSTRUCTION and nothing else — "customerName" below is still mapped by name,
#     by assignment, onto the object that expression returned. What cannot be assigned afterwards
#     is the expression's to fill, and the generated method's <remarks> lists exactly which.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label
Accept: application/json

### 1h2. AND IT CANNOT BE PROJECTED, which the build already said as an INFO:
#
#         info SM0015: the map from 'Invoice' to 'InvoiceLabelDto' builds its destination with
#                      ConstructUsing, so ProjectTo cannot use it; Map is unaffected
#
#     This request asks for the projection anyway. A projection reaches EF as one expression it
#     reads all the way down, and there is no general way to graft mapped properties onto an
#     object a delegate returned — so the generator emits a projection that THROWS, naming the
#     map and what to do instead, rather than no projection at all. (No projection would be a
#     CS0103 in the generated file the moment another map nested this one.)
#
#     The way out, when a map has to project, is the one request 1f uses: a constructor
#     ShiftMapper can match by name, plus ForMember for the arguments convention cannot work out.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label?project=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1i. MAPFROMSOURCE — letting the conversion table write the conversion.
#
#     "lineCount" is TEXT and Invoice.Lines.Count is an int. Plain MapFrom cannot express that:
#     its expression must return the DESTINATION member's type, so before this the only way was
#
#         .ForMember(d => d.Total, opt => opt.MapFrom(s => s.Lines.Sum(...).ToString("0.00")))
#
#     which is what the receipt's Total used to be. One call, and three losses: it hand-writes the
#     conversion this library exists to write, it takes the member out of the conversion table's
#     hands so SM0002/8/9/10 stop being reported for it, and ToString with no format provider
#     reads the machine's culture — so the total left a German server as "1596,00", in a library
#     whose ValueConverter exists to make exactly that impossible.
#
#     MapFromSource hands the value over instead:
#
#         LineCount = ValueConverter.ToInvariantString(...Value<..., int>("LineCount")(source))
#
#     and the projection gets the conversion as a lambda Compose splices onto the expression
#     rather than invoking, so EF still sees one expression. "agree" checks both halves match.
#
#     WATCH THE TWO TOTALS RENDER DIFFERENTLY: 1428.00 against 1428.0000, with agree still true.
#     Same decimal, different SCALE — EF writes CAST([Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [UnitPrice],
#     so SQL multiplies scale 2 by scale 2 and gets 4 where C# gets 2. That is why Total is a
#     decimal and LineCount carries the demonstration: convert a computed decimal to text and the
#     scale it happens to have becomes the string.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/2/receipt
Accept: application/json

### 1j. CONDITION — the request that could not be written before it.
#
#     mapper.Map(patch, brand) assigns every mapped member, every time. JSON has no way to say
#     "absent", so this body arrives with Name = "", ISOCode = "" and FoundedYear = 0 — and
#     without a Condition the generated update writes all three over the tracked entity.
#
#     AppMapper guards each member on the incoming value. The rule is uniform for the three
#     strings, so it is said once with ForAllMembers (see 1n) and FoundedYear keeps a TYPED one
#     of its own:
#
#         .ForAllMembers(opt => opt.Condition((s, d, value) => value is not string text || text.Length > 0))
#         .ForMember(d => d.FoundedYear, opt => opt.Condition((s, d, value) => value > 0))
#
#     Either spelling generates the same guard in the update overload:
#
#         {
#             var value = source.Name;
#             if (Customizations.Condition("Name", source, destination, destination.Name, value))
#                 destination.Name = value;
#         }
#
#     Think of it as a runtime opt.Ignore(): Ignore decides once at build time, Condition decides
#     per object. Nothing else about the member changed — it still matches by name, still goes
#     through the same conversion, still reports the same diagnostics.
#
#     The response returns "before", "after" and "sent" so the three fields that survived are
#     visible next to the blanks that would have overwritten them.
#
#     THE TRADE, which the build states rather than leaving you to find:
#
#         warning SM0017: the map from 'BrandPatch' to 'Brand' assigns 'Name', 'Country',
#                         'ISOCode', 'FoundedYear' behind a Condition, so ProjectTo cannot use
#                         it; Map is unaffected
#
#     A projection is one member initializer handed to the database, and there is no way to leave
#     a binding out per row. That is why a Condition belongs on a WRITE map like this one and not
#     on the read maps the list endpoints project.
PATCH {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "country": "Ireland"
}

### 1j2. Read brand 1 back — the name, ISO code and founded year are the ones 1j did not send,
#     and they are untouched.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 1k. CONVERTUSING — the expression IS the map, and the one map-level hook that projects.
#
#     CreateMap<Brand, BrandLabelDto>()
#         .ConvertUsing(b => new BrandLabelDto { Label = b.Name + " (" + b.ISOCode + ")" });
#
#     Not one member is matched. BrandLabelDto.Label has no counterpart on Brand and never
#     produces an SM0001, because this map does not do conventions at all. Where ConstructUsing
#     (request 1h) replaces only CONSTRUCTION and lets the members be mapped onto what it
#     returned, this replaces the lot.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/labels
Accept: application/json

### 1k2. AND IT REACHES THE DATABASE, which is the whole reason it matters:
#
#         SELECT [b].[Name] + N' (' + [b].[ISOCode] + N')' AS [Label]
#         FROM [Brands] AS [b]
#         ORDER BY [b].[Id]
#
#     One expression, two columns. Compare it with 1h2, where ConstructUsing REFUSES to project.
#
#     THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT ARBITRARY. A projection is one expression handed to SQL, so anything
#     that needs a STATEMENT cannot be in one — and that is three of the four map-level hooks:
#
#         ConstructUsing   a call whose result is then assigned onto    (SM0015, info)
#         Condition        an `if` around one binding                   (SM0017, warning)
#         BeforeMap/AfterMap  a statement of your own                   (SM0018, warning)
#         ConvertUsing     ...is already an expression                  projects
#
#     That is why ConvertUsing is the one this library is built around: a conversion registered
#     once for a TYPE PAIR is a ConvertUsing declared somewhere else, and it is worth nothing to
#     a list endpoint unless it reaches SQL.
#
#     One more thing it will not let you do quietly: anything ELSE configured on such a map does
#     nothing, so the build says so rather than letting it look configured —
#
#         warning SM0019: the map from 'X' to 'Y' uses ConvertUsing, which replaces the whole
#                         map, so its ForMember / AfterMap does nothing
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/labels?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1m. AFTERMAP — the case a ForMember cannot cover.
#
#     Look at "display". It is derived from the FINISHED destination: "label", which only the
#     ConstructUsing factory knows, plus "customerName", which only the member mapping knows.
#     No MapFrom could produce it, because a MapFrom sees the SOURCE and this needs the result.
#
#         .ForMember(d => d.Display, opt => opt.Ignore())
#         .AfterMap((s, d) => d.Display = d.Label + " — " + d.CustomerName);
#
#     THE IGNORE IS THE PATTERN, not boilerplate. A hook is an Action the generator cannot see
#     inside, so it has no idea Display gets filled, and "'InvoiceLabelDto.Display' is not
#     mapped" would be a TRUE statement about the conventions. Ignoring it says out loud which
#     members the hook owns — which is what opt.Ignore() has always meant.
#
#     BeforeMap is the other half of the pair and runs at the other end. On an update it sees the
#     object exactly as you passed it; on a create ShiftMapper moves every member it can assign
#     afterwards OUT of the object initializer so that "before" means what it says — only what
#     construction settles (constructor arguments, init-only and required members) is already
#     there.
#
#     Both are IN-MEMORY ONLY and the map loses its projection (SM0018). This one had already
#     lost it to ConstructUsing (SM0015), and BOTH are reported, because removing one would not
#     give the projection back.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label
Accept: application/json

### 1n. FORALLMEMBERS — one rule instead of the same line four times.
#
#     The BrandPatch map used to carry four Conditions, three of them identical. Now:
#
#         .ForAllMembers(opt => opt.Condition((s, d, value) => value is not string text || text.Length > 0))
#         .ForMember(d => d.FoundedYear, opt => opt.Condition((s, d, value) => value > 0))
#
#     The value arrives as an OBJECT, because one predicate has to serve members of every type.
#     That is the price of saying it once — and it is exactly why FoundedYear keeps a condition
#     of its OWN: "blank" for a number is 0, not an empty string, and a TYPED predicate says that
#     far better than a cast inside a blanket rule would.
#
#     A MEMBER'S OWN CONDITION WINS, never both. This request sends blanks for all four, so the
#     three strings are refused by the blanket rule and the year by its own.
PATCH {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/4
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "country": "",
  "isoCode": "",
  "foundedYear": 0
}

### 1n2. And a real year IS accepted, which is the typed rule doing its job — the blanket one
#     would have taken 0 just as happily, because 0 is not a blank string.
PATCH {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/4
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foundedYear": 1888
}

### 1k. FLATTENING — reading VALUES out of a graph rather than mapping objects.
#
#     InvoiceLineFlatDto carries the product, its brand and its stock BESIDE the line instead of
#     nested inside it — the shape a grid or a CSV export wants. The whole map is one line, with
#     no option at all, because flattening is ON by default:
#
#         CreateMap<InvoiceLine, InvoiceLineFlatDto>();
#
#     Every name is split on its PascalCase boundaries and walked into the source:
#
#         ProductName       -> Product.Name
#         ProductBrandName  -> Product.Brand.Name
#         ProductStockCity  -> Product.Stock.City
#         ProductPrice      -> Product.Price, AND converted to text on the way, because a
#                              flattened leaf goes through the same table a direct match uses
#
#     Compare it with request 2, the same data NESTED: that one needs a CreateMap for every type
#     in the graph, and this one needs none. It is not mapping objects at all — it is reading
#     values out of them.
#
#     IT IS ON BY DEFAULT, as it is in AutoMapper. Turn it off with o.Flattening = false, per map
#     or for a whole mapper in ConfigureDefaults, and these members go back to being SM0001.
#
#     It is still a GUESS — nothing in the name ProductName says it means Product.Name rather
#     than a column nobody has added yet — so every member it fills is reported WITH THE PATH:
#
#         info SM0020: 'InvoiceLineFlatDto.ProductBrandName' is filled by flattening,
#                      from 'InvoiceLine.Product.Brand.Name'
#
#     Informational, so a normal build stays quiet; raise it where the maps matter with
#     dotnet_diagnostic.SM0020.severity = warning.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/lines/flat
Accept: application/json

### 1k2. THE SQL, and the request to read for what is NOT in it:
#
#         SELECT [i].[Id], [i].[Quantity], [i].[UnitPrice],
#                [p].[Name] AS [ProductName], [p].[Sku] AS [ProductSku],
#                CONVERT(varchar(100), [p].[Price]) AS [ProductPrice],
#                [b].[Name] AS [ProductBrandName], [s].[City] AS [ProductStockCity]
#         FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i]
#         INNER JOIN [Products] AS [p] ON [i].[ProductId] = [p].[Id]
#         INNER JOIN [Brands]   AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId]   = [b].[Id]
#         INNER JOIN [Stocks]   AS [s] ON [p].[StockId]   = [s].[Id]
#
#     ONE query, three joins, eight columns. Two absences are the interesting part:
#
#       * NO CASE. Every step of this chain is a REQUIRED navigation (the entity declares them
#         `= null!`), so no null guard is emitted. A step declared NULLABLE would get one
#         — `source.X == null ? default(string)! : source.X.Y` — which translates to a
#         CASE WHEN and runs in memory too. Guarding a required relationship would change its
#         SQL to defend against a null the model says cannot happen.
#       * NO [Brands].[Country]. Nothing on the DTO asks for it, so it is not read. That is the
#         same thing ProjectTo has always bought, reaching two levels down a chain nobody
#         wrote an Include for.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/lines/flat?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1k3. THE TWO THINGS FLATTENING WILL NOT DO — both edits rather than requests.
#
#     (a) IT WILL NOT WALK INTO A STRING.
#
#     Add `public int ProductNameLength { get; set; }` to InvoiceLineFlatDto and it stays
#     unmapped, reported as SM0001, rather than quietly becoming Product.Name.Length. That is
#     the surprise every flattening mapper is remembered for, and a ForMember says it better.
#     A collection and a nullable value type are excluded for the same reason: there is no one
#     traversal the generator could pick.
#
#     (b) TWO PATHS IS A QUESTION, NOT A TIE TO BREAK.
#
#     If a source carried both `Order` (with a CustomerName) and `OrderCustomer` (with a Name),
#     then OrderCustomerName resolves two ways — and ShiftMapper maps neither:
#
#         warning SM0021: 'X.OrderCustomerName' is not mapped because flattening resolves it
#                         more than one way: Order.CustomerName, OrderCustomer.Name
#
#     Same answer SM0007 gives two source names differing only by case. Name it with ForMember.
#
#     NAMING CONVENTIONS are the other half of this step and have no natural home in this
#     schema, so they live in the tests: o.RecognizePrefixes("Db") lets a destination `Name` be
#     filled by a source `DbName`, and o.RecognizePostfixes("Id") lets `Customer` be filled by
#     `CustomerId`. The bare name is always tried first, so a source declaring both is not
#     ambiguous, and both apply to every step of a flattened path.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/lines/flat
Accept: application/json

### 1p. INCLUDE + INCLUDEBASE — one TPH table, and the two features it needs.
#
#     The rows behind this come from ONE table with a Discriminator column: two physical items,
#     two digital ones. db.CatalogItems is an IQueryable<CatalogItem>, so the static type says
#     nothing about which row is which — and that is the whole problem.
#
#     WITHOUT Include, every one of these would map to a bare CatalogItemDto and the weights and
#     sizes would be dropped IN SILENCE. Nothing in the types is wrong; the map for CatalogItem
#     ran, and it was correct as far as it could see.
#
#     Read "kinds" in the response: PhysicalItemDto, PhysicalItemDto, DigitalItemDto,
#     DigitalItemDto. The generated map tests the runtime type first, per element:
#
#         if (source is PhysicalItem derived0) return MapToPhysicalItemDto(derived0);
#         if (source is DigitalItem  derived1) return MapToDigitalItemDto(derived1);
#
#     THEN READ "sku" AND "kind", which are INCLUDEBASE rather than Include. Both are configured
#     ONCE, on the base map:
#
#         CreateMap<CatalogItem, CatalogItemDto>()
#             .ForMember(d => d.Sku,  opt => opt.MapFrom(s => s.Sku.ToUpper()))
#             .ForMember(d => d.Kind, opt => opt.Ignore());
#
#         CreateMap<PhysicalItem, PhysicalItemDto>().IncludeBase<CatalogItem, CatalogItemDto>();
#         CreateMap<DigitalItem,  DigitalItemDto>().IncludeBase<CatalogItem, CatalogItemDto>();
#
#     Every SKU comes back upper-cased and every "kind" is still "item", and neither derived map
#     says a word about either. WHAT IS INHERITED IS THE CONFIGURATION, not the members — the
#     members already matched by name, because PhysicalItem already IS a CatalogItem. What could
#     not be shared before was everything said ABOUT them.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog
Accept: application/json

### 1p1b. MULTI-LEVEL — the same request, read for the THIRD row type.
#
#      The catalogue is three levels deep: BundleItem derives from PhysicalItem, which derives
#      from CatalogItem. Two things only show up at this depth, and both are in the response to
#      request 1p above.
#
#      FIRST, "kinds" ends with BundleItemDto, and the last item carries "itemCount". Look at how
#      the Includes are DECLARED in AppMapper — the grandchild is LAST:
#
#          CreateMap<CatalogItem, CatalogItemDto>()
#              .Include<PhysicalItem, PhysicalItemDto>()
#              .Include<DigitalItem,  DigitalItemDto>()
#              .Include<BundleItem,   BundleItemDto>();
#
#      A BundleItem IS a PhysicalItem. Type tests are checked IN THE ORDER THEY ARE WRITTEN, so
#      emitted in declaration order `is PhysicalItem` would catch the bundle and answer it with a
#      PhysicalItemDto — itemCount silently gone. Which is exactly the failure Include exists to
#      prevent, reachable by nothing worse than writing two calls in the obvious order.
#
#      So the generator sorts the tests DEEPEST-FIRST, and the generated file is the same whichever
#      order you wrote them in. It is the rule C# enforces for catch clauses — except sorting is
#      kinder than an error here, because these calls can be spread across parts of the class and
#      there is no one place to read to get the order right.
#
#      SECOND, that bundle's "sku" is "APL-BNDL", upper-cased. Its map says only this:
#
#          CreateMap<BundleItem, BundleItemDto>().IncludeBase<PhysicalItem, PhysicalItemDto>();
#
#      It never mentions CatalogItem, where the Sku expression and the Kind Ignore were written.
#      INCLUDEBASE IS TRANSITIVE, merging nearest-first all the way up — which is what lets a
#      middle map override one member and pass everything else down untouched.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog
Accept: application/json

### 1p2. THE REFUSAL, and it is deliberate. The map above dispatches on the runtime type, so it
#      has no projection — which the build already said, as a warning, when you compiled:
#
#        warning SM0024: the map from 'CatalogItem' to 'CatalogItemDto' dispatches on the
#                        source's runtime type through Include, so ProjectTo cannot use it;
#                        Map is unaffected
#
#      A projection has ONE element type, fixed when the query is written. SQL returns rows of
#      one shape; there is no per-row type test a provider could translate and no way to hand
#      back a different DTO per row from a single SELECT.
#
#      So ShiftMapper generates a projection member that THROWS. The alternative would have been
#      to quietly return bare CatalogItemDtos — the wrong answer wearing the right type, and
#      exactly the divergence between Map and ProjectTo this library exists to prevent.
#
#      The message names what to write instead, and 1p3 is that.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/projected
Accept: application/json

### 1p3. WHAT TO WRITE INSTEAD — and it is not a workaround, it is the query you meant.
#
#          db.CatalogItems.OfType<PhysicalItem>().ProjectTo<PhysicalItemDto>(mapper)
#
#      OfType names the one shape, so the projection has an element type again. Still ONE round
#      trip, and the code now says which shape it meant.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/physical
Accept: application/json

### 1p4. THE SQL BEHIND 1p3 — the request to read carefully, for two lines in it:
#
#        SELECT COALESCE(CONVERT(varchar(100), [c].[WeightKg]), '') AS [WeightKg],
#               [c].[Id], [c].[Name], UPPER([c].[Sku]) AS [Sku]
#        FROM [CatalogItems] AS [c]
#        WHERE [c].[Discriminator] = N'PhysicalItem'
#        ORDER BY [c].[Id]
#
#      WHERE [Discriminator] = N'PhysicalItem' is what OfType compiled to — one query, not a
#      filter in memory.
#
#      UPPER([c].[Sku]) IS THE ONE TO DWELL ON. That expression was written on the BASE map, and
#      everything you write is stored against the type pair you wrote it for — so it lives
#      under CatalogItem → CatalogItemDto, and this projection is for PhysicalItem →
#      PhysicalItemDto. Asking under its own pair would have found nothing.
#
#      Both backends therefore walk a LINEAGE to resolve it. Had only the in-memory path done so,
#      Map would upper-case the SKU and ProjectTo would not — two answers that each look
#      perfectly right on their own, which is the class of bug that survives review.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/physical?sql=true

### 1p4b. THE SAME PROJECTION, ONE LEVEL DEEPER — and the strongest test the lineage gets.
#
#          db.CatalogItems.OfType<BundleItem>().ProjectTo<BundleItemDto>(mapper)
#
#      BundleItem is the third level: BundleItem → PhysicalItem → CatalogItem. Its map names
#      its PARENT and nothing else:
#
#          CreateMap<BundleItem, BundleItemDto>().IncludeBase<PhysicalItem, PhysicalItemDto>();
#
#      The Sku expression lives on CatalogItem → CatalogItemDto — TWO pairs above the one being
#      projected. Nothing about it is stored under BundleItem → BundleItemDto.
#
#      Compare the "sku" you get here with the bundle's "sku" in request 1p, which came from the
#      IN-MEMORY path. Both are "APL-BNDL". That agreement is the whole point: the two backends
#      resolve inherited configuration by walking the same lineage, so neither can drift from the
#      other as a family gets deeper.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/bundles
Accept: application/json

### 1p4c. THE SQL — one row, one query, and the leaf of the hierarchy:
#
#        SELECT COALESCE(CONVERT(varchar(11), [c].[ItemCount]), '') AS [ItemCount],
#               COALESCE(CONVERT(varchar(100), [c].[WeightKg]), '') AS [WeightKg],
#               [c].[Id], [c].[Name], UPPER([c].[Sku]) AS [Sku]
#        FROM [CatalogItems] AS [c]
#        WHERE [c].[Discriminator] = N'BundleItem'
#        ORDER BY [c].[Id]
#
#      WHERE [Discriminator] = N'BundleItem' is OfType, and it is a WHERE clause rather than a
#      filter in memory — the database returns one row, not five.
#
#      UPPER([c].[Sku]) travelled two levels of IncludeBase to get into this statement.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/bundles?sql=true

### 1p5. AS — an interface destination, and the contrast with Include that makes both worth
#      having.
#
#          CreateMap<PhysicalItem, ICatalogLabel>().As<PhysicalItemDto>();
#
#      ICatalogLabel has no constructor, so this pair was SM0004 and no map at all. As names the
#      type that stands in for it, and what comes out is a REDIRECTION rather than a second copy
#      of the mapping — Map<ICatalogLabel> is one line calling MapToPhysicalItemDto. One place
#      the mapping lives, one place to change it.
#
#      "runtimeType" in the response is PhysicalItemDto, declared as ICatalogLabel.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/labels
Accept: application/json

### 1p6. AND UNLIKE INCLUDE, AS PROJECTS. This is the distinction, and the reason both exist.
#
#      Nothing is decided per row here. The concrete type was fixed when the CreateMap was
#      written, so the projection is the concrete map's own expression with a widening cast on
#      the end:
#
#          MapCustomizations.Widen<PhysicalItem, PhysicalItemDto, ICatalogLabel>(...)
#
#      Compare this SQL with 1p4's. It is the same SELECT, discriminator and all — the database
#      was never told anything changed.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/labels?sql=true

### 1p7. OPEN GENERICS — one CreateMap, closed for every pair.
#
#      There is no CreateMap<PagedResult<PhysicalItem>, PagedResultDto<PhysicalItemDto>> anywhere
#      in AppMapper. There is only this, once:
#
#          CreateMap(typeof(PagedResult<>), typeof(PagedResultDto<>));
#
#      The generator closed it over every pair the mapper already maps — sixteen of them in this
#      sample. The alternative is that same line written once per DTO, until somebody adds a DTO
#      and forgets.
#
#      The closed maps are ORDINARY in every respect: "items" went through the element pair's own
#      map, so each one still carries its inherited upper-cased SKU, and "total" converted
#      int -> string by the ordinary conversion table.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/paged
Accept: application/json

### 1p8. THE SECOND closed map from that SAME one line — over Brand, a pair declared hundreds of
#      lines earlier with nothing whatever to do with the catalogue.
#
#      This is what "closed over every pair you already map" buys, and why the rule is that one
#      rather than something wider. Closing over every closed type in the compilation would mean
#      guessing which of a program's thousands of types somebody meant to wrap — and would
#      change its answer when an unrelated using was added.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog/paged-brands
Accept: application/json

### 1q. PROFILES — the same request as 1p, and nothing about it changed.
#
#      That is the point. Every catalogue map moved out of AppMapper's constructor into
#      Mapping/CatalogProfile.cs, added with one line:
#
#          AddProfile<CatalogProfile>();
#
#      AppMapper's constructor was 638 lines. A profile is a place to WRITE declarations, not a
#      second mapper: these are still AppMapper's maps, and there is no CatalogProfile.Map to
#      call. The response below is byte-for-byte what request 1p returned before the split.
#
#      Note what crossed the boundary without being asked to. The open generic at the foot of
#      CatalogProfile closes over Brand → BrandDto, declared in AppMapper itself
#      (/api/catalog/paged-brands still works), and the IncludeBase chain resolves across both
#      files. A profile is a place to write, not a wall.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/catalog
Accept: application/json

### 1q2. A PROFILE WITH A DEPENDENCY — the half that needed designing rather than moving text.
#
#      Mapping/InvoiceLabelProfile.cs takes IInvoiceNumbering:
#
#          public InvoiceLabelProfile(IInvoiceNumbering numbering) =>
#              CreateMap<Invoice, InvoiceLabelDto>()
#                  .ConstructUsing(s => new InvoiceLabelDto(numbering.Prefix + s.Number));
#
#      It CANNOT be built while AppMapper's constructor runs: a mapper's Services is assigned by
#      AddShiftMapper AFTER the constructor returns, because the object has to exist before
#      anything can be set on it. So AddProfile only records the type, and profiles are built the
#      first time something is actually mapped — by which point DI is in place.
#
#      The "IQ/" in the label below is that having worked: the prefix came from a service, through
#      a profile, into a map declared in a different file from the mapper that owns it.
#
#      One consequence worth knowing: a profile taking dependencies makes the WHOLE mapper
#      DI-only, because all profiles are built together and one that cannot be built fails the
#      first map. Skipping it would leave its members quietly unfilled, which is the divergence
#      this library exists to prevent — so it throws, naming the profile and what to register.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label
Accept: application/json

### 1r. GLOBAL TYPE-PAIR CONVERSIONS — one rule, written once, for every map.
#
#      Everything before this configures a MEMBER of a MAP. A ForMember is written per member per
#      map, so a rule that really belongs to a TYPE gets written as many times as the type is used
#      — and forgotten once. This is written once:
#
#          CreateConversion<DateTime, string>(
#              memory: issued => issued.Year + "/" + issued.Month + "/" + issued.Day,
#              query:  issued => issued.Year + "/" + issued.Month + "/" + issued.Day);
#
#      InvoiceStampDto below configures NOTHING. Its map is one line, it says not a word about
#      dates, and IssuedAt still comes back formatted — because the pair converts now, everywhere,
#      including in maps written tomorrow by people who never saw the rule.
#
#      It plugs into the same resolver that already knows int -> string, so it also applies through
#      collections, dictionaries and nested maps. And a registered pair BEATS the built-in table:
#      long -> string already converts, so a rule for that pair — which is exactly what a hash-id
#      rule is — would otherwise be ignored in silence.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/stamps
Accept: application/json

### 1r2. THE SQL, and the reason the query form is an EXPRESSION rather than a delegate:
#
#        SELECT [i].[Id], [i].[Number],
#               CAST(DATEPART(year,  [i].[IssuedAt]) AS nvarchar(max)) + N'/' +
#               CAST(DATEPART(month, [i].[IssuedAt]) AS nvarchar(max)) + N'/' +
#               CAST(DATEPART(day,   [i].[IssuedAt]) AS nvarchar(max)) AS [IssuedAt]
#        FROM [Invoices] AS [i]
#
#      THE FORMATTING IS IN THE STATEMENT. The generated projection carries a MARKER where the
#      conversion belongs, and Compose replaces it with the registered tree INLINED around the
#      member. Had it invoked a delegate instead, the answer would have been identical and the SQL
#      would not — EF cannot see inside a delegate, so it would have loaded every invoice and
#      formatted in C#.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/stamps?sql=true

### 1r3. AND THE OTHER HALF: a conversion registered with NO QUERY FORM.
#
#          CreateConversion<Brand, string>(memory: brand => Fingerprint(brand));
#
#      The fingerprint is a hash computed character by character. There is nothing it could say
#      about SQL, so omitting the query form is a DECLARATION rather than an oversight: this pair
#      cannot be translated.
#
#      In memory it works like any other conversion — this request maps five products and each
#      one's whole Brand becomes a hash string.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/fingerprints
Accept: application/json

### 1r4. ASK FOR THE PROJECTION ANYWAY, and it is refused — by a map that configures nothing and
#      never mentioned the conversion.
#
#      THE BUILD ALREADY SAID SO, which is the entire reason to do this at compile time:
#
#        warning SM0030: the map from 'Product' to 'ProductFingerprintDto' converts 'Brand' to
#                        'String' with a conversion that has no query form, so ProjectTo cannot
#                        use it; Map is unaffected
#
#      A runtime-only conversion table converts just as well and cannot tell you which of your list
#      endpoints has quietly stopped being one query. This is a warning on a line you can click.
#
#      It is a WARNING rather than the INFO that ConstructUsing gets, and the difference is who
#      chose: whoever wrote the CreateConversion made a decision about a type pair, and whoever
#      writes a map that happens to touch that pair inherits the consequence without asking for it.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/fingerprints?project=true
Accept: application/json

### 1s. RULES FROM A REFERENCED ASSEMBLY — the whole point of Phase 3, in two requests.
#
#      ShiftFramework.Mock is a separate project, referenced the way a NuGet package would be: a
#      compiled assembly, no analyzer, no source. This project declares NO conversion for these
#      pairs, adds no profile for them, and never names the framework's conversion class. All of it
#      arrives through two assembly attributes over there:
#
#          [assembly: ShiftMapperContract(1)]
#          [assembly: ShiftMapperConversions(typeof(ShiftEntityConversions))]
#
#      A PROFILE COULD NOT HAVE DONE THIS. A generator sees a reference as METADATA, and metadata
#      has no method bodies — so CreateConversion calls compiled into a package are simply not
#      there to read. The sample registers ShiftFileProfile on purpose so the build says exactly
#      that (SM0028). Attributes, signatures and names are what survives.
#
#      "externalIds" below is a List<long> on the entity and a List<string> here, converted by the
#      framework's hash-id rule. Note that long → string ALREADY converts — 10010 would have been
#      "10010" — so this is also the declared-beats-built-in rule doing its job.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/hashed
Accept: application/json

### 1s2. AND IT IS IN THE SQL:
#
#        SELECT [b].[Id], [b].[Name], N'H' + CAST(CAST([e].[value] AS bigint) AS nvarchar(max))
#        FROM [Brands] AS [b]
#        OUTER APPLY OPENJSON([b].[ExternalIds]) AS [e]
#
#      That N'H' travelled from another assembly, as an expression tree, through an attribute, into
#      SQL Server. The MEMORY form of the same pair is called directly by name in the generated
#      code — no lookup, no delegate — which makes the metadata route faster than declaring the
#      same rule in this project, as well as the only one that crosses an assembly.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/hashed?sql=true

### 1s3. THE OTHER RULE THE SAME PACKAGE SHIPS, and the more interesting one.
#
#      Brand.Files is a JSON column; BrandFilesDto.Files is a List<ShiftFileDTO>. The framework
#      parses it with System.Text.Json, which no database can do — so it declared that pair with a
#      MEMORY FORM AND NO QUERY FORM, which says in metadata "this cannot be projected".
#
#      In memory it works perfectly: two files, with names and urls, out of one column.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/files
Accept: application/json

### 1s4. ASK FOR THE PROJECTION AND IT IS REFUSED — and the BUILD said so first:
#
#        warning SM0030: the map from 'Brand' to 'BrandFilesDto' converts 'string?' to
#                        'List<ShiftFileDTO>' with a conversion that has no query form, so
#                        ProjectTo cannot use it; Map is unaffected
#
#      THIS IS THE ARGUMENT FOR DOING ANY OF THIS AT COMPILE TIME. A runtime conversion table
#      converts this pair just as well, and cannot tell an application which of its list endpoints
#      has quietly stopped being one query. Here a package's decision about a type pair becomes a
#      warning on a line you can click, in a project that never mentioned the pair.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/files?project=true
Accept: application/json

### 1t. A MEMBER-SHAPED CONVENTION — the last piece, and the one an AfterMap cannot do.
#
#      This project's entire involvement is one line:
#
#          CreateMap<Product, ProductListDto>();
#
#      ProductListDto has two ShiftEntitySelectDTO members, Brand and Stock. NOTHING here fills
#      them. ShiftFramework declared one rule, once, in its own profile:
#
#          CreateMemberConvention<ShiftEntitySelectDTO>()
#              .NameFrom<ShiftEntityKeyAndNameAttribute>(nameof(ShiftEntityKeyAndNameAttribute.Text))
#              .Fill(d => d.Value, "{Member}ID")
#              .FillIfPossible(d => d.Text, "{Member}.{NameOf}");
#
#      WHY A CONVERSION COULD NOT DO THIS. A conversion is handed one value and asked what it
#      becomes. This needs TWO source members, and which two depends on the destination member's
#      own NAME — Product.BrandId and Product.Brand.Name for a member called Brand.
#
#      AND {NameOf} IS WHY ONE RULE COVERS EVERYTHING. It means "the member this type nominates in
#      its own [ShiftEntityKeyAndName]". The framework never lists the entities — the entities
#      answer for themselves, and one calling its display member Title is served just as well.
#
#      LOOK AT THE TWO MEMBERS IN THE RESPONSE. They are NOT the same shape, from the same one rule:
#
#          "brand": { "value": "1", "text": "Apple" }      <- Brand nominates a name
#          "stock": { "value": "1", "text": "" }           <- Stock nominates none: id only
#
#      That is FillIfPossible: an entry DROPPED when its path does not resolve, rather than one that
#      fails the member. Often only the id is set and the label is filled in by whatever renders it.
#      With a required Fill that is SM0034 and an unmapped member, and ShiftFramework would need a
#      SECOND rule for every entity that leaves its label to the UI — which is the thing
#      conventions exist to avoid. (The empty "text" is ShiftEntitySelectDTO's own default; the
#      mapper wrote nothing there.)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/list
Accept: application/json

### 1t2. AND IT IS IN THE SQL, which is the entire point of the step:
#
#        SELECT [p].[Id], [p].[Name],
#               CONVERT(varchar(11), [p].[BrandId]) AS [Value], [b].[Name] AS [Text],
#               CONVERT(varchar(11), [p].[StockId]) AS [Value]
#        FROM [Products] AS [p]
#        INNER JOIN [Brands] AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId] = [b].[Id]
#        ORDER BY [p].[Id]
#
#      AND THERE IS NO JOIN TO [Stocks]. The id-only member reads the foreign key that is already on
#      the row, so the dropped entry costs a join as well as a column — an AfterMap filling the
#      same member could not have avoided either, because it could not appear here at all.
#
#      The member-init is inside the SELECT, and EF worked out the joins from the navigations the
#      rule walked. Written as an AfterMap — which is how this is normally done — the same rule
#      would work in memory and disappear from this query entirely, so the list would need a second,
#      hand-inlined code path. Removing that split is what the whole step is for.
#
#      Note CONVERT(varchar(11), [p].[BrandId]): the Value went through the ORDINARY conversion
#      table on its way in. That is also where a global conversion would apply — the runtime tests
#      get "H7" here rather than "7", because the same package declares hash ids and neither rule
#      mentions the other.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/list?sql=true

### 1t3. THE SAME RULE, READ BACKWARDS — the request side.
#
#      A picker posts back what it was given: a select DTO with the id in Value. Mapping it onto the
#      entity has to set Product.BrandId, and it does, from the entry written for the RESPONSE:
#
#          .Fill(d => d.Value, "{Member}ID")
#
#            becomes, going the other way:
#
#          destination.BrandId = ValueConverter.Parse<int>(source.Brand.Value, ...);
#
#      THE WRITE DIRECTION IS DERIVED, NOT DECLARED. A Fill whose path is a plain member reverses on
#      its own. The Text entry does not, and should not: a display name is read from the related row,
#      never written back to it — note that "text" below is ignored entirely.
#
#      AND THE NAVIGATION BESIDE THE KEY IS LEFT ALONE. Product.Brand name-matches the request's
#      Brand, so without the convention claiming it the build would demand a map from
#      ShiftEntitySelectDTO to Brand — an error on every write map a framework has. In the response
#      "brand" and "stock" come back null, and that is the point: you set the key, the related row is
#      the database's business.
#
#      Expect: "brandId": 2, "stockId": 3, "brand": null, "stock": null.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/products/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Test Widget",
  "sku": "TW-1",
  "price": 9.5,
  "brand": { "value": "2", "text": "ignored on the way in" },
  "stock": { "value": "3" }
}

### 2. Invoices — THE MAP AS A DATABASE QUERY.
#     db.Invoices.ProjectTo<InvoiceDto>(mapper), and again not one Include, yet the response
#     is four levels deep: invoice -> lines -> product -> brand and stock.
#
#     In the JSON: "total" is a number the entity never stores, "number" carries the "IQ/"
#     prefix from an injected service, and each line's "lineTotal" comes from the NESTED map's
#     own MapFrom — a customization two levels down that still translates.
#
#     ProjectTo returns an IQueryable, so the endpoint's OrderByDescending and Take(20) are
#     part of that same one query rather than filtering in memory afterwards.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 2b. THE SQL ITSELF — the request to read carefully.
#
#         DECLARE @p int = 20;
#         DECLARE @_numbering_Prefix nvarchar(4000) = N'IQ/';
#
#         SELECT ... FROM (SELECT TOP(@p) ..., (
#             SELECT COALESCE(SUM(CAST([i0].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i0].[UnitPrice]), 0.0)
#             FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i0] WHERE [i].[Id] = [i0].[InvoiceId]) AS [c],
#             @_numbering_Prefix + [i].[Number] AS [c0] FROM [Invoices] AS [i] ...) AS [i2]
#         LEFT JOIN (SELECT ..., CAST([i1].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i1].[UnitPrice] ...
#             FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i1]
#             INNER JOIN [Products] AS [p] ON [i1].[ProductId] = [p].[Id]
#             INNER JOIN [Brands]   AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId]   = [b].[Id]
#             INNER JOIN [Stocks]   AS [s] ON [p].[StockId]   = [s].[Id] ...) AS [s0] ...
#
#     Four levels of joins nobody asked for — EF worked them out from the map, because the
#     whole nested graph reached it as ONE expression. Three things worth finding:
#
#       * the correlated subquery for Total, from a MapFrom on THIS map;
#       * Quantity * UnitPrice, from a MapFrom on the NESTED InvoiceLine map;
#       * @_numbering_Prefix — an injected service, read once in C# because it does not depend
#         on any row, and sent as a parameter named after the field it came from.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 3. One invoice — THE SAME MAP, IN MEMORY. mapper.Map<InvoiceDto>(invoice).
#     One declaration in AppMapper feeds this and request 2 both, and they give the same
#     answer. What differs is who does the work: here the entity is loaded first and mapped in
#     C#, which is why every Include in this endpoint is load-bearing — drop the ThenInclude
#     for Brand and the DTO's brand arrives null, because there was nothing in memory to map.
#
#     This is the form for when you already have the entity: after a save, inside a unit of
#     work, or anywhere the object is in hand rather than in the database.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1
Accept: application/json

### 3b. THE TWO RULES NESTING ADDS — both are edits rather than requests, both worth doing.
#
#     (a) A NESTED OBJECT WITH NO MAP IS A BUILD ERROR.
#
#     Comment out CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>() in AppMapper.cs and the build STOPS:
#
#         error SM0011: 'InvoiceLineDto.Product' needs a map from 'Product' to 'ProductDto'.
#                       Add CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>(), or
#                       CreateMap<ProductDto, Product>().ReverseMap(), or
#                       .Ignore(d => d.Product) to leave it unmapped on purpose.
#
#     Either direction counts — a pair registered by ReverseMap satisfies it exactly as well.
#     This is the only ERROR ShiftMapper reports: every other message describes a property
#     left unmapped, which may well be on purpose, while this one describes a property that
#     cannot be mapped YET, where a null in the response would look just like a null in the
#     database.
#
#     (b) A LOOP IS ALSO A BUILD ERROR.
#
#     There is no depth setting. Mapping goes as deep as the maps you declared and stops,
#     which is the whole rule. The one graph that cannot work that way is one that points back
#     at itself — give BrandDto a List<ProductDto> Products (ProductDto already has a BrandDto
#     Brand) and the build stops:
#
#         error SM0012: nested mapping never finishes — BrandDto.Products ->
#                       ProductDto.Brand -> BrandDto. Break the loop with
#                       .Ignore(d => d.Brand) on whichever side is the back-reference.
#
#     An error rather than a silent cut, because code that followed the loop would recurse
#     until the stack ran out — and in .NET that kills the process rather than failing one
#     request.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 4. Create an invoice
#    Only product id + quantity are sent; the server snapshots the current price.
#    The response is the created invoice with the full related graph.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Layla Ibrahim",
  "customerEmail": "layla.ibrahim@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 },
    { "productId": 10, "quantity": 2 },
    { "productId": 23, "quantity": 4 }
  ]
}

### 5. Read back what request 4 created — it is mapped by the same CreateMap, so the
#     response has the full nested graph. Adjust the id to the one request 4 returned
#     (4 on a fresh database).
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/4
Accept: application/json

### 6. Not found — asking for an invoice that does not exist returns 404
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/9999
Accept: application/json

### 7. Validation — empty lines returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "No Lines",
  "customerEmail": "no.lines@example.com",
  "lines": []
}

### 8. Validation — unknown product id returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Bad Product",
  "customerEmail": "bad.product@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 99999, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}
